ource>Epileptic Disord</source><year>2004</year><volume>6</volume><fpage>267</fpage><lpage>270</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15634623</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>KM</given-names></name><name><surname>Poland</surname><given-names>RE</given-names></name><name><surname>Lesser</surname><given-names>IM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ethnicity and psychopharmacology</article-title><source>Cult Med Psychiatry</source><year>1986</year><volume>10</volume><fpage>151</fpage><lpage>165</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2872996</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Piazzini</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Canevini</surname><given-names>MP</given-names></name><name><surname>Maggiori</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Canger</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The perception of memory failure in patients with epilepsy</article-title><source>Eur J Neurol</source><year>2001</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>613</fpage><lpage>620</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11784346</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sapolsky</surname><given-names>RM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Glucocorticoids and hippocampal atrophy in neuropsychiatric disorders</article-title><source>Arch Gen Psychiatry</source><year>2000</year><volume>57</volume><fpage>925</fpage><lpage>935</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11015810</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Efficacy of zonisamide monotherapy. The percentage reduction in seizure frequency from baseline to the end of the treatment period was measured in 60 patients. Seizure freedom occurred in 45% of patients, and the reduction of seizure frequency by &#x02265; 50% was observed in 78% of patients.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jcn-3-175-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Efficacy of zonisamide monotherapy against various seizure types. All seizure types except complex partial seizure were well controlled by zonisamide. Seventy-seven percent of patients with idiopathic generalized epilepsy became seizure-free with zonisamide.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jcn-3-175-g002"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Characteristics of epilepsy patients receiving zonisamide for a minimum of 6 months (<italic>n</italic>=60)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jcn-3-175-i001"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Adverse events with zonisamide according to duration of treatment (<italic>n</italic>=60)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jcn-3-175-i002"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p><sup>*</sup>During the short-term treatment, 54 of patients (90%) reported adverse events.</p><p><sup>&#x02020;</sup>During the long-term treatment, 48 of patients (80%) reported adverse events.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>